C = [
("h1", "4. Damascus, Philistia, Moab, Ammon and Elam"),
("p", "Claim: the nations nearest to Israel received sentences with their own terms and endings, and the text sorts them. Damascus has a sentence and no clause of return. The Philistines have a sentence, a remnant given to the LORD, and a coast given to Judah. Moab, Ammon and Elam each have a sentence and, in Jeremiah, a clause that brings the captivity again “in the latter days.” In the cases of Moab and Ammon another prophet states the sentence in terms that seem to leave no room for the clause. The volume puts the statements side by side as written and does not smooth them into a timeline the text does not give."),
("sub", "4.1 Damascus"),
("p", "Three prophets speak to Damascus. Amos charges it with the threshing of Gilead and sentences it to fire, the breaking of its bar, and exile: “the people of Syria shall go into captivity unto Kir, saith the LORD” (Amos 1:5). Isaiah’s burden is shorter: “Behold, Damascus is taken away from being a city, and it shall be a ruinous heap” (Isaiah 17:1). He joins it to Ephraim: “The fortress also shall cease from Ephraim, and the kingdom from Damascus, and the remnant of Syria” (Isaiah 17:3). Jeremiah’s lines are in the voice of rumour reaching a city: “Damascus is waxed feeble, and turneth herself to flee, and fear hath seized on her” (Jeremiah 49:24), and “I will kindle a fire in the wall of Damascus, and it shall consume the palaces of Ben-hadad” (Jeremiah 49:27)."),
("p", "No clause of return is attached to Damascus in any of the three. The volume therefore reads the sentence on Damascus as closed in its own terms. One later text must be named. Zechariah 9:1 opens a burden in the land of Hadrach and says “Damascus shall be the rest thereof.” The verse names the city again, in a word that moves down the coast (Tyrus, Zidon, Ashkelon, Gaza, Ekron, Ashdod) and ends in Judah’s deliverance (Zechariah 9:1–8). The volume reads Zechariah 9 with Tyre in chapter 6 and does not decide here whether the naming of Damascus is a further stroke or only the place from which the word starts. It reports the verse and does not build on it."),
("sub", "4.2 Philistia"),
("p", "Amos charges Gaza with the sale of a whole captivity to Edom and promises fire on the wall of Gaza, the cutting off of the inhabitant from Ashdod and the sceptre from Ashkelon, and the LORD’s hand turned “against Ekron: and the remnant of the Philistines shall perish” (Amos 1:6–8). Jeremiah’s oracle has a date inside it, “before that Pharaoh smote Gaza” (Jeremiah 47:1), and the figure is of waters: “waters rise up out of the north, and shall be an overflowing flood” (Jeremiah 47:2). The sword of the LORD is addressed, “how long will it be ere thou be quiet?” and it answers itself, “How can it be quiet, seeing the LORD hath given it a charge against Ashkelon, and against the sea shore? there hath he appointed it” (Jeremiah 47:6, 7). Zephaniah says, “Gaza shall be forsaken, and Ashkelon a desolation: they shall drive out Ashdod at the noon day, and Ekron shall be rooted up” (Zephaniah 2:4)."),
("p", "Here the oracle begins to carry a clause. The sentence goes on, “O Canaan, the land of the Philistines, I will even destroy thee, that there shall be no inhabitant” (Zephaniah 2:5). Then: “And the coast shall be for the remnant of the house of Judah; they shall feed thereupon: in the houses of Ashkelon shall they lie down in the evening: for the LORD their God shall visit them, and turn away their captivity” (Zephaniah 2:7). The clause that restores belongs to Judah, and it is Judah’s captivity that is turned. Zechariah adds a different note. After the fall of Ashkelon, Gaza and Ekron, “he that remaineth, even he, shall be for our God, and he shall be as a governor in Judah, and Ekron as a Jebusite” (Zechariah 9:7). A remnant of the Philistines is taken in to the LORD’s people. The volume reads the coast given to Judah as a sentence on Philistia that closes, and the remnant of Zechariah 9:7 as a clause that no later passage reports done, and so open."),
("sub", "4.3 Moab"),
("p", "Moab has the longest run. Isaiah 15 is a lamentation: “Because in the night Ar of Moab is laid waste, and brought to silence” (Isaiah 15:1). The prophet weeps for the cities (15:2–8) and the pitch of the grief is stated, “My heart shall cry out for Moab” (Isaiah 15:5). Chapter 16 holds the clause that reaches furthest: after the call to send the lamb to “the mount of the daughter of Zion” (Isaiah 16:1) and to hide the outcasts, it says, “And in mercy shall the throne be established: and he shall sit upon it in truth in the tabernacle of David, judging, and seeking judgment, and hasting righteousness” (Isaiah 16:5). A throne in the tabernacle of David appears in the middle of the burden of Moab. The next verses return to the sentence: “We have heard of the pride of Moab” (Isaiah 16:6)."),
("p", "Isaiah 16 closes on a statement about its own layers. “This is the word that the LORD hath spoken concerning Moab since that time. But now the LORD hath spoken, saying, Within three years, as the years of an hireling, and the glory of Moab shall be contemned, with all that great multitude; and the remnant shall be very small and feeble” (Isaiah 16:13, 14). The text separates an older word from a new one and attaches a term to the new, “within three years.” It is one of the oracles that carry a clock. Jeremiah 48 takes up Moab at length: “Woe unto Nebo! for it is spoiled” (Jeremiah 48:1), the ground (“trusted in thy works and in thy treasures,” 48:7), the figure of Moab at ease, who “hath settled on his lees, and hath not been emptied from vessel to vessel” (48:11), and the end, “Moab shall be destroyed from being a people, because he hath magnified himself against the LORD” (48:42). Then, at the close of the chapter: “Yet will I bring again the captivity of Moab in the latter days, saith the LORD. Thus far is the judgment of Moab” (Jeremiah 48:47)."),
("p", "Zephaniah states the end of Moab in terms that sound like the opposite. “Surely Moab shall be as Sodom, and the children of Ammon as Gomorrah, even the breeding of nettles, and saltpits, and a perpetual desolation: the residue of my people shall spoil them, and the remnant of my people shall possess them” (Zephaniah 2:9). Both statements are in the LORD’s voice, and the volume does not say which governs. It reports that Zephaniah 2:9 says the land is a perpetual desolation possessed by the remnant of Israel, and Jeremiah 48:47 says the captivity of Moab is brought again in the latter days. No passage joins the two, and the volume joins them with no theory. It holds Jeremiah 48:47 as a clause of restoration that no later passage reports done, and it holds Zephaniah 2:9 beside it. The words “Thus far is the judgment of Moab” (Jeremiah 48:47) mark where Jeremiah’s own account of the sentence stops."),
("sub", "4.4 Ammon"),
("p", "Amos charges Ammon with the ripping of the women of Gilead “that they might enlarge their border” (Amos 1:13). Jeremiah 49 begins with a question about land: “Hath Israel no sons? hath he no heir? why then doth their king inherit Gad, and his people dwell in his cities?” (Jeremiah 49:1). The sentence is that Rabbah “shall be a desolate heap, and her daughters shall be burned with fire: then shall Israel be heir unto them that were his heirs, saith the LORD” (Jeremiah 49:2). Jeremiah gives the ground, “O backsliding daughter? that trusted in her treasures, saying, Who shall come unto me?” (Jeremiah 49:4), and then the clause: “And afterward I will bring again the captivity of the children of Ammon, saith the LORD” (Jeremiah 49:6)."),
("p", "Ezekiel says a thing that sits uneasily beside it. The LORD will deliver Ammon “to the men of the east for a possession” and “I will make Rabbah a stable for camels, and the Ammonites a couching place for flocks” (Ezekiel 25:4, 5). The sentence continues, “Unto the men of the east with the Ammonites, and will give them in possession, that the Ammonites may not be remembered among the nations” (Ezekiel 25:10). The words “that the Ammonites may not be remembered among the nations” and “I will bring again the captivity” come from two prophets, and neither prophet qualifies the other. The volume reads them as two statements about Ammon in the LORD’s voice. It does not claim to know what the second does to the first, and the clause of Jeremiah 49:6 remains one that no later passage reports done."),
("sub", "4.5 Elam"),
("p", "Elam appears once at length. The word came to Jeremiah “in the beginning of the reign of Zedekiah king of Judah” (Jeremiah 49:34). The LORD will “break the bow of Elam, the chief of their might” (Jeremiah 49:35), will bring “the four winds from the four quarters of heaven” and scatter them so that “there shall be no nation whither the outcasts of Elam shall not come” (Jeremiah 49:36), and will say, “I will set my throne in Elam, and will destroy from thence the king and the princes” (Jeremiah 49:38). The last verse, and the last verse of the oracle, is the clause: “But it shall come to pass in the latter days, that I will bring again the captivity of Elam, saith the LORD” (Jeremiah 49:39). Elam is named in the cup list of Jeremiah 25:25, and its one oracle has a sentence, a scattering and a return."),
("sub", "4.6 “In the latter days”"),
("p", "The three clauses of Jeremiah (48:47; 49:6; 49:39) share a phrase for the time of the return, and the Authorized Version translates the same Hebrew phrase by two English forms, “last days” (Isaiah 2:2) and “latter days.” The volume notes the phrase and draws no date from it. It does mean that the clause is not stated as “afterward” in the sense of next year. The word for Ammon is “afterward” (Jeremiah 49:6), for Moab and Elam it is “in the latter days.” The text sets Moab and Elam on a longer horizon than the sentence itself."),
("p", "Peels, writing on the whole collection of Jeremiah 46 to 51, counts these clauses and uses them against the thought that the oracles were hidden comfort for Israel: “four times in Jeremiah 46-51 a word of welfare is added to a word of judgment on a foreign nation” (//European Journal of Theology// 16:2, 2007). He lists them as Jeremiah 46:26, 48:47, 49:6 and 49:39. The count helps the volume’s sorting in one respect. The clauses are few, and they are placed on particular nations; the other oracles of Jeremiah 46 to 51 have none. A nation with a clause is a different case from a nation without one."),
("ctbl", "The nearest nations in four questions.", [
    ("Nation", "Ground and sentence", "Clause of restoration", "Class"),
    ("Damascus", "Threshing of Gilead; taken away from being a city (Amos 1:3–5; Isaiah 17:1; Jeremiah 49:23–27)", "None in Amos, Isaiah or Jeremiah; Zechariah 9:1 names the city again", "Closed in its terms"),
    ("Philistia", "Sale of captives; no inhabitant; coast given to Judah (Amos 1:6–8; Jeremiah 47; Zephaniah 2:4–7)", "A remnant “for our God” (Zechariah 9:7); the turned captivity in Zephaniah 2:7 is Judah’s", "Closed, with an open remnant"),
    ("Moab", "Pride and trust in treasures; three-year term; a perpetual desolation (Isaiah 15–16; Jeremiah 48; Zephaniah 2:9)", "“Yet will I bring again the captivity of Moab in the latter days” (Jeremiah 48:47)", "Open"),
    ("Ammon", "Ripping of the women of Gilead; not remembered among the nations (Amos 1:13; Ezekiel 25:2–10; Jeremiah 49:1–5)", "“Afterward I will bring again the captivity” (Jeremiah 49:6)", "Open"),
    ("Elam", "Bow broken; four winds; throne set in Elam (Jeremiah 49:34–38)", "“In the latter days … bring again the captivity of Elam” (Jeremiah 49:39)", "Open"),
  ], [0.14, 0.38, 0.3, 0.18]),
("sub", "4.7 What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Damascus has a sentence in three prophets and no clause of return; Zechariah 9:1 names the city again and the volume reports the verse without building on it.",
    "The Philistines are sentenced to desolation, their coast is given to Judah, and a remnant is taken into the LORD’s people (Zephaniah 2:5–7; Zechariah 9:7).",
    "Moab has a term of three years in Isaiah 16:14, a perpetual desolation in Zephaniah 2:9, and a clause of return in the latter days in Jeremiah 48:47; the volume holds the three as written.",
    "Ammon is given “in possession, that the Ammonites may not be remembered among the nations” in Ezekiel 25:10 and has the captivity brought again in Jeremiah 49:6; the two stand together.",
    "Elam has one oracle and one clause in the latter days (Jeremiah 49:34–39); the clauses of Jeremiah 48:47, 49:6 and 49:39 are the open ones among these nations.",
  ]),
]
